      "dom_id": "question-22753",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Write $2x+22y=-8$ in standard form.",
      "worked_solution": "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nThe equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $2.$ So, we divide by $2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+22y & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{2x+22y}{2} & =\\frac{-8}{2} \\\\ x+11y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+11y=-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
